    "case_id": "26",
    "case_summary": "A 27-year-old nulliparous Caucasian female presented to the Gynaecology Unit with a 7-month history of pelvic pain and absence of menstruation. She also reported stress incontinence, chronic constipation, and recurrent intestinal and urinary infections. Her medical history included several congenital malformations, many of which required surgical intervention. Neonatally, she underwent anoplasty for an anorectal malformation with a rectovestibular fistula. At two months old, a laparotomy revealed hydronephrosis due to obstruction of the ectopic right ureteral orifice, leading to megadolichoureter and recurrent urinary infections. A cutaneous ureterostomy was performed to improve renal function. Two months later, another laparotomy was performed following urography that showed decreased renal function, resulting in a right nephrectomy. Histological findings indicated chronic pyelonephritis. Additional conditions included right hand thumb torsion of the metacarpal-phalangeal joint with hypotrophy of the thenar eminence, valgus, and hemisacrum (type IV sacral malformation), and bladder dysfunction treated with medication. A standard karyotype on peripheral blood cells showed a normal female karyotype (46 XX) with no chromosomal abnormalities. An ultrasound in our department revealed a complex mass originating from the right pelvis extending to the right hypochondrium and epigastrium, with a transverse extension of over 20 cm. Fluid was observed in the Douglas pouch. Abdominal and pelvic CT confirmed an irregular polylobate complex mass measuring 24 × 19.5 × 13.5 cm; the uterus and adnexa were not identified. Chest X-ray and CT scans were negative for pleural effusion or lung metastasis.\n\nHistological examination of the pelvic mass exhibited multiple smooth muscle-like nodules with low cellularity, no cytological atypia and few mitoses. Immunohistochemical evaluation was strongly positive for desmin and actin, oestrogen receptor (ER) and progesterone receptor (PR), but negative for cytokeratin and EMA (Epithelial Membrane Antigen). Cytological examination of the peritoneal liquid revealed only reactive mesothelial cells and foamy histiocytes.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
